 "audit": "Several features lowered confidence. (1) Extreme hypermutators (about 20 cases, up to 15,731 mutations) were excluded from testing; they would create spurious gene-diagnosis links. (2) Many diagnosis labels are vague or overlapping (NOS, malignant glioma, 'Neoplasm, malignant', 'missing', 'Unknown'), and related labels (Rhabdomyosarcoma NOS vs embryonal, medulloblastoma subtypes, ependymoma subtypes) are split, which dilutes frequencies and makes 'other tumors' heterogeneous; no subgroup information (e.g. medulloblastoma SHH/WNT) is available. (3) Per-case mutation burden differs strongly by diagnosis (e.g. rhabdomyosarcoma median >17 vs pilocytic astrocytoma ~2), suggesting differing sequencing or calling pipelines or cohort sources, which can confound gene associations. (4) Some strong statistical hits were rejected as annotation artifacts because protein positions exceed the protein length (NOTCH2 in embryonal rhabdomyosarcoma, NF2 in pilocytic astrocytoma, BCOR in medulloblastoma; positions like p.E127469G); I did not report them. MED12 in ependymoma has plausible positions but a scattered, non-hotspot pattern, so it is reported with low probability as not established. (5) Small groups limit power; the cohort probably pools studies with different capture and filtering, and clinical fields (site, age) are incomplete.",
 "methods": "Excluded cases with >150 non-synonymous mutations, counted each case once per gene, and for every diagnosis with at least 8 cases tested every gene mutated in at least 3 cases of that diagnosis against all other cohort cases with two-sided Fisher exact tests, keeping only enrichments. I inspected protein_change positions and hotspot patterns (H3 K28M, BRAF V600E, IDH1 R132, CTNNB1 exon 3, FGFR4 N535/V550, NRAS Q61) to remove annotation artifacts and confirm biological plausibility. Strong, hotspot-supported and literature-consistent associations got high probabilities; weaker ones were assigned lower probabilities, and frequencies are observed fractions in the diagnosis shrunk slightly."
